1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:01,874
Five-thirty in the morning,

2
00:00:01,874 --> 00:00:03,262
nineteen fifty-five.

3
00:00:03,442 --> 00:00:04,556
It's still dark.

4
00:00:04,736 --> 00:00:06,873
In a thousand kitchens across town,

5
00:00:06,873 --> 00:00:08,582
a light comes on at the same

6
00:00:08,582 --> 00:00:09,009
moment.

7
00:00:09,189 --> 00:00:10,838
Coffee is percolating.

8
00:00:11,018 --> 00:00:13,165
Lunch pails are being packed.

9
00:00:13,345 --> 00:00:14,772
And down by the river,

10
00:00:14,772 --> 00:00:16,459
behind a chain-link fence,

11
00:00:16,459 --> 00:00:18,988
the smokestacks of the mill are already

12
00:00:18,988 --> 00:00:21,194
breathing white into the cold air.

13
00:00:21,543 --> 00:00:23,471
This is a factory town.

14
00:00:23,651 --> 00:00:24,854
One big employer,

15
00:00:24,854 --> 00:00:27,401
and everything else built around it.

16
00:00:27,581 --> 00:00:28,836
The steel mill,

17
00:00:28,836 --> 00:00:30,090
the paper mill,

18
00:00:30,090 --> 00:00:31,345
the auto plant,

19
00:00:31,345 --> 00:00:32,684
the glass works,

20
00:00:32,684 --> 00:00:34,106
the textile mill.

21
00:00:34,286 --> 00:00:36,053
In the nineteen fifties,

22
00:00:36,053 --> 00:00:39,071
millions of Americans lived in towns like

23
00:00:39,071 --> 00:00:42,164
this, from Pennsylvania to Michigan to the

24
00:00:42,164 --> 00:00:42,900
Carolinas.

25
00:00:43,250 --> 00:00:45,543
Today we're going to spend one whole

26
00:00:45,543 --> 00:00:47,835
day there, from the first whistle in

27
00:00:47,835 --> 00:00:49,872
the morning to the last light in

28
00:00:49,872 --> 00:00:51,145
the tavern at night.

29
00:00:51,325 --> 00:00:53,025
The work, the houses,

30
00:00:53,025 --> 00:00:55,938
the families, the union hall and the

31
00:00:55,938 --> 00:00:56,828
ball field.

32
00:00:57,008 --> 00:00:58,993
This is what life in a nineteen

33
00:00:58,993 --> 00:01:01,362
fifties factory town was really like.

34
00:01:01,712 --> 00:01:02,729
In the kitchen,

35
00:01:02,729 --> 00:01:04,627
mother packs the lunch pail.

36
00:01:04,807 --> 00:01:08,780
Two sandwiches, usually bologna or leftover meatloaf

37
00:01:08,780 --> 00:01:09,927
on white bread.

38
00:01:10,107 --> 00:01:10,722
An apple.

39
00:01:10,902 --> 00:01:13,528
A slice of pie wrapped in wax

40
00:01:13,528 --> 00:01:14,071
paper.

41
00:01:14,251 --> 00:01:16,083
And a thermos of hot coffee,

42
00:01:16,083 --> 00:01:18,176
clipped into the curved lid of a

43
00:01:18,176 --> 00:01:20,596
black metal pail that her husband has

44
00:01:20,596 --> 00:01:22,297
carried for fifteen years.

45
00:01:22,647 --> 00:01:24,421
Father eats standing up,

46
00:01:24,421 --> 00:01:25,898
in his work clothes.

47
00:01:26,078 --> 00:01:27,158
Heavy boots.

48
00:01:27,338 --> 00:01:29,579
Denim or canvas trousers.

49
00:01:29,759 --> 00:01:30,939
A flannel shirt,

50
00:01:30,939 --> 00:01:32,046
even in summer,

51
00:01:32,046 --> 00:01:34,701
because the sparks and the hot metal

52
00:01:34,701 --> 00:01:36,841
don't care what season it is.

53
00:01:37,021 --> 00:01:38,336
He kisses his wife,

54
00:01:38,336 --> 00:01:40,758
rubs the head of the youngest child

55
00:01:40,758 --> 00:01:42,558
still asleep at the table,

56
00:01:42,558 --> 00:01:44,149
and walks out the door.

57
00:01:44,499 --> 00:01:46,345
Most of the men walk to work.

58
00:01:46,525 --> 00:01:49,161
The houses were built close to the

59
00:01:49,161 --> 00:01:50,479
plant on purpose,

60
00:01:50,479 --> 00:01:53,967
decades earlier, often by the company itself.

61
00:01:54,147 --> 00:01:56,824
Rows of narrow frame houses on steep

62
00:01:56,824 --> 00:02:00,317
streets, with small front porches and backyards

63
00:02:00,317 --> 00:02:03,068
just big enough for a clothesline and

64
00:02:03,068 --> 00:02:04,480
a vegetable garden.

65
00:02:04,831 --> 00:02:07,299
The streets fill with men walking in

66
00:02:07,299 --> 00:02:08,602
the same direction,

67
00:02:08,602 --> 00:02:10,042
lunch pails swinging,

68
00:02:10,042 --> 00:02:12,168
cigarettes glowing in the dark.

69
00:02:12,348 --> 00:02:14,971
Fathers, sons, brothers,

70
00:02:14,971 --> 00:02:16,063
neighbors.

71
00:02:16,243 --> 00:02:17,630
In a lot of families,

72
00:02:17,630 --> 00:02:20,469
three generations worked at the same plant.

73
00:02:20,649 --> 00:02:22,754
The grandfather on the pension,

74
00:02:22,754 --> 00:02:24,383
the father on the floor,

75
00:02:24,383 --> 00:02:26,420
and the son just hired on last

76
00:02:26,420 --> 00:02:26,895
spring.

77
00:02:27,246 --> 00:02:29,807
At six forty-five the whistle blows,

78
00:02:29,807 --> 00:02:32,226
loud enough to hear all over town.

79
00:02:32,406 --> 00:02:35,225
Everybody in town sets their clocks by

80
00:02:35,225 --> 00:02:35,448
it.

81
00:02:35,628 --> 00:02:37,559
The men line up at the time

82
00:02:37,559 --> 00:02:40,062
clock, find their card in the rack,

83
00:02:40,062 --> 00:02:40,992
and punch in.

84
00:02:41,172 --> 00:02:43,184
One minute late is still late,

85
00:02:43,184 --> 00:02:44,794
and the foreman notices.

86
00:02:45,145 --> 00:02:48,186
Inside, the noise is tremendous.

87
00:02:48,366 --> 00:02:49,794
Machines pounding.

88
00:02:49,974 --> 00:02:51,286
Furnaces roaring.

89
00:02:51,466 --> 00:02:54,543
Overhead cranes rumbling along their tracks.

90
00:02:54,723 --> 00:02:55,841
In a steel mill,

91
00:02:55,841 --> 00:02:58,288
the heat near the open hearth could

92
00:02:58,288 --> 00:03:00,386
make a man's face feel like it

93
00:03:00,386 --> 00:03:01,225
was on fire.

94
00:03:01,405 --> 00:03:03,590
The men talk with hand signals,

95
00:03:03,590 --> 00:03:06,269
or shout right into each other's ears.

96
00:03:06,619 --> 00:03:07,696
It was hard work,

97
00:03:07,696 --> 00:03:09,660
and it could be dangerous work.

98
00:03:09,840 --> 00:03:11,483
Most men had a scar,

99
00:03:11,483 --> 00:03:13,126
a missing fingertip,

100
00:03:13,126 --> 00:03:15,262
or a story about the day a

101
00:03:15,262 --> 00:03:16,412
cable snapped.

102
00:03:16,592 --> 00:03:17,972
Safety rules existed,

103
00:03:17,972 --> 00:03:20,075
but they were looser than today.

104
00:03:20,255 --> 00:03:23,203
Hard hats and goggles were becoming common,

105
00:03:23,203 --> 00:03:24,780
but earplugs were rare,

106
00:03:24,780 --> 00:03:26,906
and many of those men grew hard

107
00:03:26,906 --> 00:03:28,277
of hearing by fifty.

108
00:03:28,627 --> 00:03:30,523
And yet, the men were proud of

109
00:03:30,523 --> 00:03:31,471
what they made.

110
00:03:31,651 --> 00:03:33,671
The steel in the new bridges.

111
00:03:33,851 --> 00:03:35,960
The glass in the windows of the

112
00:03:35,960 --> 00:03:36,777
new suburbs.

113
00:03:36,957 --> 00:03:39,848
The cars on every highway in America.

114
00:03:40,028 --> 00:03:42,354
When a man drove past a skyscraper

115
00:03:42,354 --> 00:03:42,970
or a dam,

116
00:03:42,970 --> 00:03:44,817
he could tell his children,

117
00:03:44,817 --> 00:03:47,075
our mill made the steel for that.

118
00:03:47,425 --> 00:03:49,154
The foreman ran the floor.

119
00:03:49,334 --> 00:03:51,315
He was usually an older man who

120
00:03:51,315 --> 00:03:53,232
had come up through the ranks,

121
00:03:53,232 --> 00:03:55,149
worked every job in the plant,

122
00:03:55,149 --> 00:03:57,450
and knew every machine by its sound.

123
00:03:57,630 --> 00:03:59,706
He wore a white shirt instead of

124
00:03:59,706 --> 00:04:02,432
work clothes, and a different colored hard

125
00:04:02,432 --> 00:04:02,692
hat.

126
00:04:02,872 --> 00:04:04,602
Some foremen were feared.

127
00:04:04,782 --> 00:04:07,011
The good ones were respected for life.

128
00:04:07,361 --> 00:04:09,903
At lunchtime, the whistle blows again.

129
00:04:10,083 --> 00:04:12,715
The men sit on crates and benches,

130
00:04:12,715 --> 00:04:14,031
open their pails,

131
00:04:14,031 --> 00:04:16,585
and trade sandwiches and stories.

132
00:04:16,765 --> 00:04:17,798
Who's sick.

133
00:04:17,978 --> 00:04:19,139
Who's getting married.

134
00:04:19,319 --> 00:04:21,641
Which foreman is in a bad mood.

135
00:04:21,821 --> 00:04:23,168
And always, always,

136
00:04:23,168 --> 00:04:25,509
how the high school team is going

137
00:04:25,509 --> 00:04:27,069
to do on Friday night.

138
00:04:27,419 --> 00:04:29,174
In nineteen fifty-five,

139
00:04:29,174 --> 00:04:32,456
about one in three American workers outside

140
00:04:32,456 --> 00:04:35,128
of agriculture belonged to a union.

141
00:04:35,308 --> 00:04:36,543
In factory towns,

142
00:04:36,543 --> 00:04:39,160
it was often nearly everybody on the

143
00:04:39,160 --> 00:04:40,033
plant floor.

144
00:04:40,213 --> 00:04:42,495
The union hall was down the street

145
00:04:42,495 --> 00:04:43,502
from the plant,

146
00:04:43,502 --> 00:04:45,717
with a flag out front and folding

147
00:04:45,717 --> 00:04:46,656
chairs inside.

148
00:04:47,007 --> 00:04:49,120
The union negotiated the wages,

149
00:04:49,120 --> 00:04:51,848
the hours, the vacation and the pension.

150
00:04:52,028 --> 00:04:54,347
A contract might bring a raise of

151
00:04:54,347 --> 00:04:55,753
a few cents an hour,

152
00:04:55,753 --> 00:04:57,439
a week of paid vacation,

153
00:04:57,439 --> 00:04:59,969
and health insurance for the family.

154
00:05:00,149 --> 00:05:02,357
For a man whose own father had

155
00:05:02,357 --> 00:05:05,301
worked twelve-hour days for very little,

156
00:05:05,301 --> 00:05:07,951
those contracts felt like a miracle.

157
00:05:08,301 --> 00:05:11,193
When contracts ran out and talks broke

158
00:05:11,193 --> 00:05:13,095
down, there were strikes.

159
00:05:13,275 --> 00:05:15,694
The men walked the picket line in

160
00:05:15,694 --> 00:05:17,379
shifts, carrying signs,

161
00:05:17,379 --> 00:05:20,750
keeping warm around barrels with fires burning

162
00:05:20,750 --> 00:05:21,263
inside.

163
00:05:21,443 --> 00:05:24,230
Wives brought coffee and sandwiches.

164
00:05:24,410 --> 00:05:26,909
Strikes could last weeks or months,

165
00:05:26,909 --> 00:05:29,051
and families lived on savings,

166
00:05:29,051 --> 00:05:31,550
strike pay and help from neighbors.

167
00:05:31,900 --> 00:05:33,730
In nineteen fifty-five,

168
00:05:33,730 --> 00:05:36,514
the two biggest labor groups in the

169
00:05:36,514 --> 00:05:38,503
country, the A F of L and

170
00:05:38,503 --> 00:05:39,299
the C I O,

171
00:05:39,299 --> 00:05:40,572
merged into one.

172
00:05:40,752 --> 00:05:42,829
For the men on the plant floor,

173
00:05:42,829 --> 00:05:44,771
it was a sign of how powerful

174
00:05:44,771 --> 00:05:45,977
unions had become.

175
00:05:46,157 --> 00:05:48,289
Many of them would later say those

176
00:05:48,289 --> 00:05:50,547
were the best years for the American

177
00:05:50,547 --> 00:05:51,300
working man.

178
00:05:51,650 --> 00:05:53,764
Wages in those plants were good for

179
00:05:53,764 --> 00:05:54,308
the time.

180
00:05:54,488 --> 00:05:57,151
A steelworker or an autoworker might earn

181
00:05:57,151 --> 00:05:59,295
around two dollars an hour in the

182
00:05:59,295 --> 00:06:02,023
mid-fifties, about twice the minimum wage.

183
00:06:02,203 --> 00:06:04,529
With overtime, a man could buy a

184
00:06:04,529 --> 00:06:06,927
house, a new car every few years,

185
00:06:06,927 --> 00:06:08,889
and a cottage on a lake for

186
00:06:08,889 --> 00:06:10,051
summer weekends.

187
00:06:10,402 --> 00:06:12,933
Everything in town depended on the plant.

188
00:06:13,113 --> 00:06:14,729
When the plant was busy,

189
00:06:14,729 --> 00:06:17,153
the stores on Main Street were busy.

190
00:06:17,333 --> 00:06:20,038
The car dealer sold new Chevrolets.

191
00:06:20,218 --> 00:06:22,575
The bakery sold birthday cakes.

192
00:06:22,755 --> 00:06:24,856
The bank gave out mortgages.

193
00:06:25,036 --> 00:06:26,814
When the plant laid men off,

194
00:06:26,814 --> 00:06:28,782
the whole town felt it within a

195
00:06:28,782 --> 00:06:29,100
week.

196
00:06:29,450 --> 00:06:30,609
On every corner,

197
00:06:30,609 --> 00:06:33,000
it seemed, there was a church and

198
00:06:33,000 --> 00:06:33,652
a tavern.

199
00:06:33,832 --> 00:06:36,332
The churches often belonged to the different

200
00:06:36,332 --> 00:06:38,036
groups who had come to work at

201
00:06:38,036 --> 00:06:38,604
the plant.

202
00:06:38,784 --> 00:06:41,036
Polish, Italian, Slovak,

203
00:06:41,036 --> 00:06:44,978
Irish, German, Black families who had come

204
00:06:44,978 --> 00:06:46,667
up from the South.

205
00:06:46,847 --> 00:06:48,505
Each had its own parish,

206
00:06:48,505 --> 00:06:50,853
its own festival and its own food.

207
00:06:51,203 --> 00:06:52,840
The company was everywhere.

208
00:06:53,020 --> 00:06:55,291
It sponsored the Little League team and

209
00:06:55,291 --> 00:06:56,398
the bowling league.

210
00:06:56,578 --> 00:06:59,376
It built the community swimming pool,

211
00:06:59,376 --> 00:07:01,872
the library or the hospital wing.

212
00:07:02,052 --> 00:07:04,040
It held a picnic every summer,

213
00:07:04,040 --> 00:07:06,226
with games for the children and a

214
00:07:06,226 --> 00:07:07,683
hot dog for everybody.

215
00:07:07,863 --> 00:07:09,837
Some people loved the company.

216
00:07:10,017 --> 00:07:10,841
Some didn't.

217
00:07:11,021 --> 00:07:12,681
But nobody could ignore it.

218
00:07:13,032 --> 00:07:14,815
Women ran the town while the men

219
00:07:14,815 --> 00:07:15,818
were at the plant.

220
00:07:15,998 --> 00:07:17,473
They shopped, cooked,

221
00:07:17,473 --> 00:07:19,511
cleaned, raised the children,

222
00:07:19,511 --> 00:07:22,110
ran the church suppers and the school

223
00:07:22,110 --> 00:07:22,883
bake sales.

224
00:07:23,063 --> 00:07:24,414
Some worked too,

225
00:07:24,414 --> 00:07:25,597
in the stores,

226
00:07:25,597 --> 00:07:28,554
the offices or the plant cafeteria.

227
00:07:28,734 --> 00:07:31,565
And many women in textile towns worked

228
00:07:31,565 --> 00:07:33,352
right on the mill floor,

229
00:07:33,352 --> 00:07:34,470
beside the men.

230
00:07:34,820 --> 00:07:36,844
Children grew up with the sounds of

231
00:07:36,844 --> 00:07:38,233
the plant in their ears.

232
00:07:38,413 --> 00:07:39,168
The whistle.

233
00:07:39,348 --> 00:07:41,310
The trains rolling into the yard at

234
00:07:41,310 --> 00:07:41,646
night.

235
00:07:41,826 --> 00:07:43,748
The glow of the furnaces on the

236
00:07:43,748 --> 00:07:44,864
clouds after dark,

237
00:07:44,864 --> 00:07:47,343
which some children thought was the most

238
00:07:47,343 --> 00:07:49,141
beautiful thing in the world.

239
00:07:49,321 --> 00:07:50,721
They knew that someday,

240
00:07:50,721 --> 00:07:52,792
many of them would work there too.

241
00:07:53,142 --> 00:07:54,732
There was a price for that glow.

242
00:07:54,912 --> 00:07:56,979
On some days the air smelled of

243
00:07:56,979 --> 00:07:59,846
sulfur, and the soot settled on windowsills

244
00:07:59,846 --> 00:08:02,180
and on the laundry hanging outside.

245
00:08:02,360 --> 00:08:05,413
Mothers wiped the porch railings every morning.

246
00:08:05,593 --> 00:08:08,807
Nobody talked much about pollution in nineteen

247
00:08:08,807 --> 00:08:09,576
fifty-five.

248
00:08:09,756 --> 00:08:11,820
People said it smelled like money,

249
00:08:11,820 --> 00:08:12,914
and they meant it.

250
00:08:13,264 --> 00:08:14,348
At three o'clock,

251
00:08:14,348 --> 00:08:16,388
the whistle blows for the end of

252
00:08:16,388 --> 00:08:17,281
the day shift.

253
00:08:17,461 --> 00:08:19,761
The gates open and the men pour

254
00:08:19,761 --> 00:08:21,320
out, tired and dirty,

255
00:08:21,320 --> 00:08:23,324
as the next shift walks in.

256
00:08:23,504 --> 00:08:25,129
The plant never stopped.

257
00:08:25,309 --> 00:08:27,504
Three shifts, around the clock,

258
00:08:27,504 --> 00:08:29,628
every day of the week except a

259
00:08:29,628 --> 00:08:31,044
few holidays a year.

260
00:08:31,395 --> 00:08:33,432
Many of the men stopped at the

261
00:08:33,432 --> 00:08:35,537
tavern on the corner on the way

262
00:08:35,537 --> 00:08:35,876
home.

263
00:08:36,056 --> 00:08:38,575
One beer, sometimes two.

264
00:08:38,755 --> 00:08:40,114
A shot and a beer,

265
00:08:40,114 --> 00:08:42,076
if it had been a hard day.

266
00:08:42,256 --> 00:08:44,694
The bartender knew everybody's order.

267
00:08:44,874 --> 00:08:47,115
A ball game was on the radio,

268
00:08:47,115 --> 00:08:49,898
or on the small television above the

269
00:08:49,898 --> 00:08:52,758
bar, and the arguments about it never

270
00:08:52,758 --> 00:08:53,221
ended.

271
00:08:53,572 --> 00:08:55,864
At home, dinner was on the table

272
00:08:55,864 --> 00:08:56,938
at five-thirty.

273
00:08:57,118 --> 00:08:58,823
Meat and potatoes,

274
00:08:58,823 --> 00:09:00,149
almost always.

275
00:09:00,329 --> 00:09:03,300
Pierogi on Friday in Polish homes,

276
00:09:03,300 --> 00:09:06,447
spaghetti on Sunday in Italian ones.

277
00:09:06,627 --> 00:09:08,785
The whole family sat down together,

278
00:09:08,785 --> 00:09:11,067
and the father got the biggest piece,

279
00:09:11,067 --> 00:09:13,164
because he had worked the hardest.

280
00:09:13,344 --> 00:09:15,062
Nobody argued about that.

281
00:09:15,412 --> 00:09:17,539
After dinner, the men sat on the

282
00:09:17,539 --> 00:09:19,533
porch or worked in the garden.

283
00:09:19,713 --> 00:09:20,926
On summer evenings,

284
00:09:20,926 --> 00:09:23,417
the neighbors talked across the fences,

285
00:09:23,417 --> 00:09:25,779
and the children played in the street

286
00:09:25,779 --> 00:09:26,482
until dark.

287
00:09:26,662 --> 00:09:27,510
In the fall,

288
00:09:27,510 --> 00:09:29,843
the men went hunting on weekends.

289
00:09:30,023 --> 00:09:30,982
In the winter,

290
00:09:30,982 --> 00:09:32,969
there was the bowling league,

291
00:09:32,969 --> 00:09:34,887
the lodge, and church bingo.

292
00:09:35,238 --> 00:09:37,190
The men on the night shift slept

293
00:09:37,190 --> 00:09:38,106
during the day,

294
00:09:38,106 --> 00:09:40,058
in bedrooms with heavy curtains,

295
00:09:40,058 --> 00:09:41,950
while the rest of the town went

296
00:09:41,950 --> 00:09:42,621
on outside.

297
00:09:42,801 --> 00:09:45,522
Their children learned to play quietly in

298
00:09:45,522 --> 00:09:46,517
the afternoons.

299
00:09:46,697 --> 00:09:49,297
Their wives learned to cook breakfast at

300
00:09:49,297 --> 00:09:51,638
three in the afternoon and dinner at

301
00:09:51,638 --> 00:09:52,223
midnight.

302
00:09:52,403 --> 00:09:53,646
In a factory town,

303
00:09:53,646 --> 00:09:56,408
every family lived on the plant's clock.

304
00:09:56,759 --> 00:09:59,313
Friday was payday and game day.

305
00:09:59,493 --> 00:10:01,481
The paychecks came out at the end

306
00:10:01,481 --> 00:10:02,264
of the shift,

307
00:10:02,264 --> 00:10:04,613
and that evening the stores stayed open

308
00:10:04,613 --> 00:10:04,915
late.

309
00:10:05,095 --> 00:10:07,166
Then the whole town went to the

310
00:10:07,166 --> 00:10:09,104
high school football stadium,

311
00:10:09,104 --> 00:10:11,576
which was often named after a company

312
00:10:11,576 --> 00:10:14,182
founder, and sat in the bleachers under

313
00:10:14,182 --> 00:10:14,917
the lights.

314
00:10:15,267 --> 00:10:16,359
In mill towns,

315
00:10:16,359 --> 00:10:19,713
high school football was almost a religion.

316
00:10:19,893 --> 00:10:21,821
The players were the sons of the

317
00:10:21,821 --> 00:10:23,086
men in the bleachers.

318
00:10:23,266 --> 00:10:25,362
Many of them would be working beside

319
00:10:25,362 --> 00:10:26,993
their fathers within a year.

320
00:10:27,173 --> 00:10:28,858
And for a lot of those boys,

321
00:10:28,858 --> 00:10:31,205
Friday nights under the lights would be

322
00:10:31,205 --> 00:10:33,071
the best memory of their lives.

323
00:10:33,421 --> 00:10:34,440
After the game,

324
00:10:34,440 --> 00:10:36,955
the teenagers went to the drive-in or

325
00:10:36,955 --> 00:10:37,635
the diner.

326
00:10:37,815 --> 00:10:39,505
The parents went home,

327
00:10:39,505 --> 00:10:41,580
or to the social club for a

328
00:10:41,580 --> 00:10:42,041
dance.

329
00:10:42,221 --> 00:10:43,455
And late at night,

330
00:10:43,455 --> 00:10:45,991
the whistle blew for the shift change

331
00:10:45,991 --> 00:10:47,910
again, and a new crew of men

332
00:10:47,910 --> 00:10:49,555
walked through the gate,

333
00:10:49,555 --> 00:10:50,789
as the town slept.

334
00:10:51,140 --> 00:10:52,991
The men who did those jobs were

335
00:10:52,991 --> 00:10:53,647
not famous.

336
00:10:53,827 --> 00:10:55,895
Nobody wrote songs about them,

337
00:10:55,895 --> 00:10:57,136
at least not then.

338
00:10:57,316 --> 00:10:59,331
But in nineteen fifty-five,

339
00:10:59,331 --> 00:11:01,421
America produced more steel,

340
00:11:01,421 --> 00:11:03,884
more cars and more goods than any

341
00:11:03,884 --> 00:11:05,153
country on earth.

342
00:11:05,333 --> 00:11:07,536
And it happened because of towns like

343
00:11:07,536 --> 00:11:09,382
this one, and the men and women

344
00:11:09,382 --> 00:11:11,823
who woke up at five-thirty every morning.

345
00:11:12,173 --> 00:11:13,844
For many of those towns,

346
00:11:13,844 --> 00:11:15,725
the good years didn't last.

347
00:11:15,905 --> 00:11:18,726
Starting in the late nineteen sixties and

348
00:11:18,726 --> 00:11:21,408
seventies, plants closed or moved away.

349
00:11:21,588 --> 00:11:25,663
Foreign competition, new technology and changing companies

350
00:11:25,663 --> 00:11:28,403
emptied out mills all over the country.

351
00:11:28,583 --> 00:11:29,930
The whistles went quiet.

352
00:11:30,110 --> 00:11:32,338
And the towns built around them had

353
00:11:32,338 --> 00:11:33,930
to find a way to survive.

354
00:11:34,280 --> 00:11:35,728
But if you grew up in a

355
00:11:35,728 --> 00:11:36,673
town like that,

356
00:11:36,673 --> 00:11:37,681
you remember it.

357
00:11:37,861 --> 00:11:39,347
The sound of the whistle.

358
00:11:39,527 --> 00:11:41,520
The smell of the mill when the

359
00:11:41,520 --> 00:11:42,383
wind changed.

360
00:11:42,563 --> 00:11:44,914
Your father's lunch pail on the kitchen

361
00:11:44,914 --> 00:11:45,396
counter.

362
00:11:45,576 --> 00:11:47,343
The pride in his voice when he

363
00:11:47,343 --> 00:11:48,815
said what his plant made.

364
00:11:48,995 --> 00:11:50,981
Those towns built America.

365
00:11:51,161 --> 00:11:52,600
And the people in them knew it.

366
00:11:52,951 --> 00:11:54,866
So tell us in the comments.

367
00:11:55,046 --> 00:11:57,662
Did your father or grandfather work at

368
00:11:57,662 --> 00:11:58,901
a mill or a plant?

369
00:11:59,081 --> 00:12:00,079
What did they make?

370
00:12:00,259 --> 00:12:01,633
We read every comment,

371
00:12:01,633 --> 00:12:03,881
and we'd love to hear their stories.

372
00:12:04,061 --> 00:12:06,324
Next, we're going to a wedding in

373
00:12:06,324 --> 00:12:07,695
nineteen sixty-five.

374
00:12:07,875 --> 00:12:09,647
Thank you for spending this time with

375
00:12:09,647 --> 00:12:09,791
me.
